course: money
lesson: 27
title: Interest on savings and cash machines
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: When you keep money in a savings account, the bank pays you a little extra called interest, a thank-you for letting it use your money for a while.
explain: Interest is usually written as a percentage, said "per cent", which means "out of every 100": 3% means £3 for every £100, so when Ella leaves £100 for a year at 3% the bank adds £3, and she has £103.
explain: Leave the interest in and next year you get interest on the interest: at 10%, which is £10 for every £100, £100 becomes £110; next year the £100 earns £10 again and the extra £10 earns £1, so it becomes £121; this is compound interest, and it grows faster the longer you wait.
explain: A cash machine, or ATM, gives out cash from your account; you put in your card, cover the keypad while you type your PIN, and the money comes off your balance.
explain: Picture a cash machine with something odd stuck on the card slot: walk away and use another one, because criminals sometimes fit fake parts to steal card details.
fact: Interest on savings is extra money the bank pays you; the interest rate is the percentage it pays.
fact: Percent means "out of 100": 3% of £100 is £3, so £100 becomes £103 after a year.
fact: 5% means £5 for every £100, so £200 earns £5 + £5 = £10 and becomes £210 after a year.
fact: Compound interest is interest on interest: at 10%, £100 becomes £110 after one year, and after two years £110 + £11 = £121, because the extra £10 earns £1 as well.
fact: A cash machine, or ATM, gives out cash and takes it off your balance: £150 − £20 = £130.
fact: Some cash machines charge a fee to take money out; taking out £10 with a £1.95 fee takes £11.95 from the account.
fact: A 4-digit PIN has 10,000 possible codes, from 0000 to 9999, so covering the keypad keeps it secret from nosy people and hidden cameras.
fact: Shoulder surfing means watching someone type their PIN.
fact: A fixed-term account locks savings away for a set time but pays more: 4% on £100 is £4, compared with 2% on £100, which is £2, in an ordinary account.
fact: If prices rise faster than interest, savings buy less: at 2% interest £100 becomes £102, but a £100 toy rising 4% costs £104.
fact: A skimmer is a fake device fitted to a cash machine to steal card details; if a machine looks odd, use another and tell the bank.
fact: A card left too long in a cash machine is kept to protect you; the money in the account stays safe, and the bank sends a new card.
exercise: Ella leaves £100 in savings for a year and the bank adds £3. How much does she have now? | £103 | £3 | £300 | £130
exercise: What does "percent" mean? | Out of every 100 | Out of every 10 | Out of every 1,000 | Out of every pound you spend
exercise: What is interest on savings? | Extra money the bank pays you for keeping money there | A fee you pay the bank every year to look after your money | Money you slowly lose if you leave it in the bank too long | The money you take out of a cash machine in the high street
exercise: Harry has £200 at 5% interest. How much interest does he get in a year? | £10 | £5 | £20 | £100
exercise: After one year at 5%, what does Harry's £200 become? | £210 | £205 | £250 | £220
exercise: Grace's £100 grew to £110 at 10%. What will she have after the second year? | £121 | £120 | £111 | £220
exercise: What is compound interest? | Interest paid on the interest you already earned | Interest that is taken away from your savings every year | Interest paid only on the very first £100 you saved | Interest that only children under twelve are allowed to get
exercise: Mum has £150 and takes £20 out of a cash machine. What is her balance now? | £130 | £170 | £150 | £120
exercise: A machine charges £1.95 to take out £10. How much leaves the account? | £11.95 | £10 | £8.05 | £12.95
exercise: Why does Dad cover the keypad when typing his PIN? | So nosy people or hidden cameras can't see it | So the machine works faster and gives him his money sooner | So the bank gives him a little extra interest for being careful | So the PIN changes automatically for next time
exercise: How many different 4-digit PINs are there? | 10,000 | 4 | 9,999 | 1,000
exercise: An ordinary account pays 2% and a locked account pays 4%. How much more does the locked one give on £100 in a year? | £2 | £4 | £6 | £1
exercise: What is the catch with Nina's locked savings account? | She can't take the money out for a year | It pays less interest than an ordinary account | The bank keeps half the money at the end | She has to pay the bank every month
exercise: Kian's savings grow 2% a year, but prices rise 4%. What happens to what his £100 can buy? | It buys a bit less, because prices rose faster | It buys a lot more, because he got interest | It buys exactly the same as before | It buys nothing, because savings stop working after a while
exercise: Mia sees a strange thing stuck on a cash machine's card slot. What should she and her mum do? | Use a different machine and tell the bank | Put the card in quickly before it gets worse | Pull the thing off and keep it as a souvenir | Use the machine but type the PIN in twice to be safe
exercise: Lucy's card is swallowed by the machine. What happens to the £25 in her account? | It stays safe, and the bank sends her a new card | It is lost for ever somewhere deep inside the machine | The machine gives it to the next customer in the queue | The bank keeps it as a fine for forgetting to take it
test: Ella shouts, "Free money!" when the bank adds £3. Her brother says, "Nah, the bank's mugging you off somehow." ("Mugging you off" means tricking you.) Who's closer? | Ella: the bank pays interest because it uses her money while it's there | Her brother: banks always secretly take money back | Neither: interest is only paid to grown-ups | Her brother: the bank will charge her £3 later
test: Your mate goes, "5% of £200 is £5, easy." Is he right? | No, it's £10, because 5% means £5 for every £100 | Yes, 5% always means £5 | No, it's £50, because 5 × 10 = 50 | No, it's £205, because you add the 5
test: A cousin from the Highlands says, "Ma granny's savings went fae £100 tae £110, and the next year she got the 10% on the hale £110." ("Fae" means from; "tae" means to; "hale" means whole.) What does she have now? | £121 | £120 | £111 | £130
test: What would happen if Grace left her money in for many more years at the same rate? | It would grow faster and faster, because of interest on interest | It would grow by exactly £10 every year for ever | It would stop growing after two years | It would shrink back to £100 after a while
test: Dad says, "Nah, £1.95 to take a tenner out? We'll find a free one." Your sister says, "It's only £1.95, chill." What's the point Dad is making? | The fee is nearly a fifth of the £10, which is a lot to pay for cash | Free machines give out more money than you ask for | Machines with fees always steal your card | Fees are paid by the bank, not by Dad
test: In a game, the cash machine gives you as much money as you like, even with £0 in your account. What's wrong? | A real machine only gives money that is in your account | Real machines also give unlimited money to anyone | Real machines print new money for each customer | Nothing, as long as you know your PIN
test: Lucy forgets her card in the machine and moans, "Ugh, it ate my card, it's well peak." ("Peak" means bad luck.) Why did the machine keep it? | To protect her, so nobody else could take it | Because she had no money at all left in her account | Because the machine was hungry for plastic that day | To fine her for being far too slow at the screen
test: A grandad from Yorkshire says, "Interest is low, prices are up, me savings are going backwards, love." What does he mean? | Prices rising faster than interest means his money buys less | His savings are being taken out by the bank each month | Interest makes savings shrink when it is low | He has spent his savings on Yorkshire puddings
test: Nina's mum says, "Locked for a year, but it pays 4%." She puts in £300 — no, £200. How much interest after a year? | £8 | £12 | £4 | £204
test: Your big sister says, "Someone filmed my PIN? Nah, I don't need to cover the keypad, nobody's that sus." ("Sus" means dodgy.) What's the wise answer? | Always cover it, because cameras and nosy people can watch | She's right, nobody ever watches cash machines | Covering the keypad makes the machine refuse you | Only people with really big balances ever need to cover the keypad
test: Harry says, "Whatever, 5% or 3%, it's the same thing." On £200, how much more does 5% give than 3% in a year? | £4 | £2 | £6 | £10
exam: It is given that the sum of £200 is deposited at an interest rate of five per cent per annum. Determine the interest accruing after one year. | £10 | £5 | £20 | £100
exam: Ascertain the balance of a deposit of £100 after two years at ten per cent per annum, interest being compounded annually. | £121 | £120 | £110 | £200
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises compound interest? | Interest calculated upon the principal together with interest previously accrued | Interest calculated exclusively upon the original sum deposited | A charge levied by a bank in respect of each withdrawal | A reduction of the balance in respect of rising prices
exam: Identify the course of action most consistent with prudence where an unfamiliar device is observed affixed to the card slot of a cash machine. | Abstention from use of the machine and notification of the bank | Insertion of the card with exceptional rapidity | Removal of the device and retention of it by the customer as evidence | Entry of the personal identification number on two occasions
